Q: Redirect both cout and stdout to a string in C++ for Unit Testing A: <p><a href="http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/io/basic_stringstream" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::stringstream</code></a> may be what you're looking for.</p>
<p><strong>UPDATE</strong><br />
Alright, this is a bit of hack, but maybe you could do this to grab the printf output:</p>
<pre><code>char huge_string_buf[MASSIVE_SIZE];
freopen(&quot;NUL&quot;, &quot;a&quot;, stdout);
setbuf(stdout, huge_string_buf);
</code></pre>
<p>Note you should use &quot;/dev/null&quot; for linux instead of &quot;NUL&quot;.  That will rapidly start to fill up huge_string_buffer.  If you want to be able to continue redirecting output after the buffer is full you'll have to call fflush(), otherwise it will throw an error.  See <a href="http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/io/c/setbuf" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::setbuf</code></a> for more info.</p>

Q: A method of iterating over the values of a list or a dictionary without a type conditional A: <p>Have you tried using <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/collections.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">collections</a>?</p>
<p>Using functions for everyday situations is always a good path. Even more so when they handle possibilities and offer us possibilities for multiple uses.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code># construct
from collections.abc import Mapping

def iter_values(obj):
    if isinstance(obj, Mapping):
        return obj.values()
    return obj

# use
data1 = [1,2,3]
data2 = {&quot;a&quot;:1,&quot;b&quot;:2,&quot;c&quot;:3}

for v in iter_values(data1):
    print(v)

for v in iter_values(data2):
    print(v)
</code></pre>
